                                      next one as ORA-03120 (#9976,
                                      #9702)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-selectint-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     oci7bind --bind=selectint: "select
                                      :num from dual" with one SQLT_INT
                                      bind and one odefin, against
                                      sqlrelay's oracleprotocoloci7
                                      instance, portable encoding.  the
                                      smallest request that carries both
                                      a bind block and a define block, so
                                      it is what shows where one ends and
                                      the other begins.  the bind goes out
                                      as wire type 2 with buffer size 22
                                      and its value as the oracle internal
                                      number c1 0b (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-selectstr-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the same with the bind SQLT_STR
                                      instead - wire type 1, buffer size
                                      one less than the client's declared
                                      width, charset 31, and the value
                                      "bindstring" as raw text.  diffing
                                      it against selectint is what
                                      isolates the datatype and length
                                      fields (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-selecttwo-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     two binds and two defines, which is
                                      what gives the stride from one bind
                                      descriptor to the next - a one-bind
                                      capture alone cannot (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-insert-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the three-bind insert, with no
                                      defines at all, so the bind block
                                      sits alone with nothing behind it.
                                      options 0x8028 here against 0x8038
                                      on the selects, the difference being
                                      OPTION_DEFINE (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-nullbind-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the same insert with two of the three
                                      indicators set to -1.  a null bind
                                      is "fd 01" where a value's length
                                      byte would be, and the request still
                                      ends exactly on its last byte, which
                                      is what pins a null at two bytes
                                      rather than one or three (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-reverse-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     the same insert with the obndrv calls
                                      made backwards - :vchr, then :chr,
                                      then :num.  the values still go out
                                      in statement order (c1 33 = 50, then
                                      "revchar", then "revvarchar"), which
                                      is what settles that the nth value
                                      on the wire is the nth placeholder
                                      in the text rather than the nth
                                      obndrv call.  oci7 binds by name and
                                      the names never reach the wire, so
                                      nothing else on file could tell
                                      those two apart (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-many-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     bind once, oexec three times.  the
                                      second and third executes are not
                                      query2 requests at all - the client
                                      sends a bare TTI_EXECUTE (0x04)
                                      carrying fresh values and no
                                      descriptors, since obndrv binds by
                                      reference and only the buffer
                                      changed.  the only capture on file
                                      of that shape (#9700)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-out-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     "begin :v := 7; end;" - a pl/sql
                                      block with one out bind.  options
                                      0x0428, which is BIND, EXECUTE and
                                      SNDIOV with NOPLSQL correctly clear,
                                      and the request stops dead on the
                                      last byte of its one bind
                                      descriptor: no TTC_ROW_DATA, no
                                      value (#9700, #9984)
    9700-redhat9x86-portable-inout-sqlrelay.oraproxy
                                     "begin :v := :v * 2; end;" - byte for
                                      byte identical to the out capture
                                      above, even though this one has an
                                      initial value of 21 to pass in.  so
                                      an in-out pl/sql bind sends no more
                                      than an out-only one does, and the
                                      whole of the exchange is in the
                                      response, which no capture on file
                                      has (#9700, #9984)

Each of the 9808-*, 9810-*, 9976-* and 9700-*.oraproxy captures above is
oraproxy's own capture format (see "oraproxy" below), taken by interposing
oraproxy locally on the client host and pointing the client's tnsnames.ora
at it, rather than a raw tcpdump - a real NIC's MSS is too small for
oradecode's one-frame-one-TNS-chunk assumption to hold. oradecode reads them
with no conversion.

Every column in these four multicol-*.cap captures is a NUMBER column
defined SQLT_STR (the legacy putField() character-array arm) - only the
column count and fetch mode vary. They evidence the legacy fetch path's
row/column framing across column counts, not any additional Oracle
datatype; see #9704 for datatype coverage itself.

The three real Oracle test servers on this network
--------------------------------------------------

Not discoverable anywhere else in this tree:

    oracle122centos7x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.1:1521   Oracle 12.2
    oracle112centos5x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.2:1521   Oracle 11.2
    oracle102centos4x86 (10.0.2.3)        10.0.2.3:1521   Oracle 10.2

The first two take user testuser / password testpassword against SID
ora1 (see oracle.java). As of this writing, the 11.2 server's testuser
account has an expired password (ORA-28001) - that needs a DBA to reset
it before a full login can be captured there again; it is unrelated to
oraproxy or oradecode, both of which handle the error path correctly
regardless.

The third, oracle102, is the only one of the three old enough to speak
OCI7/O3LOGON - it's the backend the oracleprotocoloci7 test instance
uses (see test/sqlrelay.conf.d/oracleprotocol.conf.in). Its hostname is
not recorded anywhere in this tree beyond this line; only its IP,
10.0.2.3, is confirmed (#9664 comment 21).
